\[\mathcal{C}^{m}(\Omega)=\{u\in\mathcal{C}^{0}(\Omega);\,\partial^{\alpha}u\in \mathcal{C}^{0}(\Omega)\quad\forall|\alpha|\leqslant m\}.\]

As the \(\mathcal{C}^{m}\)-functions are not necessarily bounded we also introduce the space

\[\mathcal{C}^{m}(\bar{\Omega})=\{u\in\mathcal{C}^{m}(\Omega);\,\partial^{\alpha}u \text{ are bounded and uniformly continuous on }\Omega\]

\[\forall 0\leqslant|\alpha|\leqslant m\}.\]

Likewise, we define the space \(\mathcal{C}^{m,\,1}(\bar{\Omega})\):

\[\mathcal{C}^{m,\,1}(\bar{\Omega})=\{u\in\mathcal{C}^{m}(\bar{\Omega});\, \partial^{\alpha}u\text{ are Lipschitz-continuous in }\bar{\Omega}\quad\forall 0\leqslant| \alpha|\leqslant m\}.\]

For \(m\geqslant 0\), \(\mathcal{C}^{m}(\bar{\Omega})\) and \(\mathcal{C}^{m,\,1}(\Omega)\) are Banach spaces for the respective norms:

\[\|u\|_{\mathcal{C}^{m}(\bar{\Omega})}=\max_{0\leqslant|\alpha|\leqslant m} \sup_{x\in\Omega}|\partial^{\alpha}u(x)|,\]

\[\|u\|_{\mathcal{C}^{m,1}(\bar{\Omega})}=\|u\|_{\mathcal{C}^{m}(\bar{\Omega})} +\max_{0\leqslant|x|\leqslant m}\sup_{\begin{subarray}{c}x,y\in\Omega\\ x\neq y\end{subarray}}\frac{|\partial^{\alpha}u(x)-\partial^{\alpha}u(y)|}{ \|x-y\|},\]

where \(\|x\|\) denotes the Euclidean norm of \(\mathbb{R}^{N}\).

As \(\mathcal{D}(\Omega)\subset W^{m,\,p}(\Omega)\), we define

\[W^{m,\,p}_{0}(\Omega)=\overline{\mathcal{D}(\Omega)}^{W^{m,\,p}(\Omega)},\]

i.e. \(W^{m,\,p}_{0}(\Omega)\) is the closure of \(\mathcal{D}(\Omega)\) for the norm \(\|\,.\,\|_{m,\,p,\,\Omega}\). When \(m\geqslant 1\) and \(\Omega\) is a proper subset of \(\mathbb{R}^{N}\) then \(W^{m,\,p}_{0}(\Omega)\) is generally a proper subspace of \(W^{m,\,p}(\Omega)\) and we shall characterize its functions further on. On the other hand, when \(m=0\) we have the following result.

**Lemma 1.1**.: _The space \(\mathcal{D}(\Omega)\) is dense in \(L^{p}(\Omega)\) for \(1\leqslant p<\infty\)._

The next theorem, called the Poincare-Friedrichs inequality, asserts that the mapping \(v\to|v|_{m,\,\Omega}\) is a norm on \(H^{m}_{0}(\Omega)\), equivalent to \(\|\,.\,\|_{m,\,\Omega}\).

**Theorem 1.1**.: _If \(\Omega\) is connected and bounded at least in one direction, then for each integer \(m\geqslant 0\), there exists a constant \(K=K(m,\Omega)>0\) such that_

(1.4) \[\|v\|_{m,\,\Omega}\leqslant K|v|_{m,\,\Omega}\quad\quad\forall v\in H^{m}_{0} (\Omega).\]

For \(1\leqslant p<\infty\), we denote by \(W^{-m,\,p}(\Omega)\) the dual space of \(W^{m,\,p}_{0}(\Omega)\) normed by:

